{
  "id": "Compound::DB01028 - Gene::2560",
  "verbalization": "Methoxyflurane, a commonly used anesthetic, is shown to bind to Gabra4, Gabrd, and Gabre, all of which are subunits of the Gaba A receptor. These receptors are critical for the activation of the GABAergic system, which plays a key role in neuronal excitation and inhibition. The evidence suggests that Methoxyflurane may modulate GABA receptor activity through interaction with these subunits, potentially influencing neural signaling. The shared involvement of Gabrb1 in the activation of Gaba A receptors across different subunit interactions implies a broader mechanism of action, possibly through a common downstream pathway. The high scores in the paths indicate a strong, consistent relationship between Methoxyflurane and Gabrb1, supported by multiple binding and participation interactions. The mechanism appears to involve the modulation of GABA receptor function, which could have implications for neurological and psychiatric disorders. The evidence is robust and consistent, supporting a mechanistic link between Methoxyflurane and Gabrb1.",
